      "type": "single",
      "difficulty": "hard",
      "stem": "**Tai nėra nauja knyga.** The sentence is negative, yet *knyga* is nominative. Why is it not genitive?",
      "explanation": "The genitive of negation reaches a **direct object** (¶26). *būti* has no object — *nauja knyga* is a predicate nominative agreeing with the subject (¶16, ¶17) — so there is nothing for the rule to act on. Learners who have just met ¶26 tend to apply it to every negative sentence they see.",
      "rulesTested": [
        "buti-present",
        "negation-genitive",
        "predicate-nominative",
        "adjective-agreement"
      ],
      "vocabUsed": [
        "būti",
        "naujas",
        "knyga",
        "tai",
        "ne"
      ],
      "options": [
        "Because *nėra* is not transitive: there is no object for the negation to convert.",
        "Because *knyga* is feminine, and feminine nouns keep the nominative.",
        "Because the genitive of negation applies only in the past tense.",
        "Because *tai* has already taken the genitive in its place."
